IF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.

This is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.
</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>

## The Rule

**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.

**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.

Then announce "Using [skill] to [purpose]" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.

## Skill Priority

When multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.

- "Let's build X" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.
- "Fix this bug" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.

## Red Flags

These thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:

| Thought | Reality |
|---------|---------|
| "This is just a simple question" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |
| "I need more context first" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |
| "Let me explore the codebase first" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |
| "I can check git/files quickly" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |
| "Let me gather information first" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |
| "This doesn't need a formal skill" | If a skill exists, use it. |
| "I remember this skill" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |
| "This doesn't count as a task" | Action = task. Check for skills. |
| "The skill is overkill" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |
| "I'll just do this one thing first" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |
| "This feels productive" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |
| "I know what that means" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |

## Platform Adaptation

If your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:

- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`
- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`
- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`
- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`
- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`
- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`

## User Instructions

User instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.
# Global Preferences

- **ALWAYS use a git worktree for code changes.** Run `/worktree` before editing any project files. Never create branches in the main working tree — multiple sessions share it and will clobber each other's files. The only exceptions: `.claude/` files, `docs/` files, and single-file edits the user explicitly asks for in-place.
- **The main working tree stays on its default trunk (`main`).** Never checkout other branches in the main tree — all feature work happens in worktrees, including work on a development trunk like `next` (branch those worktrees from `origin/next`). After merging, worktrees are cleaned up automatically (post-merge hook runs `~/.claude/scripts/cleanup-worktrees.sh`). You can also run it manually: `bash ~/.claude/scripts/cleanup-worktrees.sh` (add `--dry-run` to preview).
- **Don't assume `main` is the only trunk.** A repo may have more than one long-lived trunk — typically a stable trunk (`main`) and a development trunk (e.g. `next`). Work out which lane a task belongs to, then pull/branch from that lane and target the PR base at it. If it isn't explicit which lane applies, **ask the user rather than defaulting to `main`**. A repo's own `CLAUDE.md` states its lanes and what each is for.
- **ALWAYS `git pull` the relevant trunk before starting any work.** The local trunk can be stale. Run `git pull origin <lane>` (the task's lane — see the two-trunk rule above) at the start of every session before branching, running scripts, or making decisions based on repo state.
- **Live verification is encouraged when deployed behavior matters.** For a fix that depends on the live server environment, a small, safe hotpatch before opening a PR is highly encouraged to confirm the change works under real deployment conditions, but it is not required. Keep the repository change as the source of truth, use local tests where they provide sufficient evidence, and label anything not verified live. Server-side OpenClaw review/fix agents must not hotpatch or deploy themselves; they make only small, directed repository fixes.

- **Subagents may be used at assistant discretion when useful.** If the environment provides subagent/task delegation tools, use them for review gates, parallel investigation, or isolated implementation work when they improve quality or speed; do not wait for the user to explicitly ask for subagents unless a higher-priority system/developer instruction forbids them.


## PR Size & Review Rounds — LAW (Paul, 2026-10-21)

Two PRs in two days reached ~200 review comments (wcpos/monorepo#2798 at 21 Codex rounds and +2,670 production lines on a 1,200-line budget; a PHP library PR the day before). Full text and the why: `rules/pr-size-and-review-rounds.mdc`. The limits, binding every model and agent:

- **A PR carries at most 600 added production lines** (tests, snapshots, docs, lockfiles, generated files excluded). Over that, split BEFORE opening; never explain the size in the body. Hook: `hooks/block-oversized-pr.js` on PR creation.
- **A budget breach is a STOP, not a note.** Report what grew and propose the split.
- **An implementer that stops at its line budget ends the slice; the orchestrator never raises a budget itself** (ruled 2026-10-21 D1 A after five sessions in three days did — 941 against 600, 1,018 against 750, 1,027 against 450, "under your 3x stop, so I kept going" — and two of those PRs were the ones Paul called overengineered). Bring him ONE question with the overrun and a trim option. The 3x stop in `rules/agent-protocol.mdc` bounds the task tree; it is not a licence to accept an overrun.
- **A brief's budget is a gate too** (ruled 2026-10-25 D1 A): the orchestrator writes the ceiling it briefed to `~/.claude/state/pr-budget/<branch>` before dispatching, and `block-oversized-pr.js` refuses a PR above it exactly as it refuses 600, until Paul's override file exists. **The brief's budget binds when the PR opens, not after** (ruled 2026-10-31 D1 A, after seven one-word "lift?" round trips in a day): once the PR is open, review-round fixes and trunk merges grow it under the 600-line law and the round-5 cap only — no lift question for post-open growth. **A ceiling the session set itself is never under 150 added production lines** (ruled 2026-11-02 D1 A, after three sessions in two days briefed 40, 50 and 120 on their own and each came back for a lift of 11–28 lines): Paul's numbers and the 600 law are untouched, and the budget file is written only for a ceiling he set or one of 150+.
- **A PR's review loop ends at round 5** (15 top-level bot threads). No sixth round of branch-by-branch fixes: write the missing invariant in one place, split the PR, or bring Paul the open list. Hook: `hooks/block-review-round-cap.js` on push.
- Only Paul lifts a limit, per PR, with the `/tmp/claude-override-*` file the hook names, and **his "lift" / "go with what you recommend" in chat IS the touch**: the session then creates the file and records his words in the budget file, never a new ceiling number (ruled 2026-10-28 D1 A). **What the session recommends depends on what the overrun is made of** (ruled 2026-10-23 D2 A, qualified 2026-10-28 D1 A after *"don't cut a feature just to save 25 lines of code"*): unasked-for code (locks, fallbacks, abstractions, scope creep) → recommend the trim or split, never the override; formatter re-wraps → counted separately and said so; an overrun that is ALL board-approved behaviour → the ONE question is "lift to N for this PR?" and the recommendation is the lift. Never name a ruled feature as the thing to cut.

## Scope of a Ruling — LAW (Paul, 2026-10-30)

On 2026-10-30 one session turned *"cashier and register modals come from the right … consistent throughout the app"* into #3118 (EVERY side panel right, the subject rule deleted, merged) and then #3123 (the `left` presentation removed from the component library, on nobody's word). Paul: *"Cashier and Register modal come from the right, but not every fucking modal."* Binding on every model and agent:

- **A ruling's scope is the nouns Paul named.** "Consistent throughout the app" / "everywhere" widens WHERE the named things apply, never WHICH things it applies to. Anything beyond the named examples is ONE question before the branch exists, never an inference recorded as his ruling.
- **A capability removal is never a consequence of a ruling.** Deleting an option, prop value, presentation, animation, hook or setting because a ruling stopped using it is a separate decision only Paul makes. "A public export with no caller is deleted" covers dead code; it is not a licence to retire what a ruling made unused, and an architect-gate or critic "recommendation" is not Paul.
- **A memory or brief that records a ruling quotes him verbatim and states the scope as the quote has it.** Never write the widened reading as the ruling; a later session reads memory as fact and merges on it.
- **Standing merge authority covers the ruled scope only.** A PR whose body says "every"/"all"/"drops"/"removes" against a ruling that named specific things is held for Paul, not merged green.

## PR Handoff Default

- **The agent that submits a PR owns its follow-up.** Monitor CI and reviewer comments, make and verify the fixes, and report any unresolved blockers; use `/pr` Phase 5 rather than handing responsibility to another agent or service.
- **ALWAYS create a PR when a coherent batch of work is finished.** Do not wait for the user to ask. Once implementation has been validated as far as possible, commit the work, push the branch, and open a PR.
- **Prefer preserving work over keeping it local.** If work is partial, blocked, interrupted, or likely to be lost between sessions, commit the current state and open a **draft PR** with a clear status, remaining TODOs, and any failing/blocked validation. This is explicitly preferred over leaving unpushed local changes in a worktree.
- **Run applicable validation before pushing and document the results.** If lint/tests/build are blocked by pre-existing tooling issues or the work is intentionally partial, still push the draft PR and record the exact commands and failures in the PR body.
- **If you believe the work is finished, the PR must be a normal ready-for-review PR, not a draft.** Draft PRs are only for partial checkpoints, incomplete work, or known failures introduced by unfinished implementation.

## Brainstorm Overrides

When running /brainstorm:
- Do NOT ask questions you can answer yourself from the codebase, docs, git history, or common sense. Answer them yourself and state your assumption.
- If the task is straightforward, skip clarifying questions entirely and go straight to proposing approaches.
- Only ask a question if it surfaces a genuinely non-obvious tradeoff or creative angle the user hasn't considered. "What framework?" when there's one in package.json is a waste of time.

## Autonomy & Stops (all models, all subagents)

Applies to the top-level session and to every Agent-tool subagent, which sees this file and not the harness prompt. Source: Addy Osmani's Anthropic guide to Opus 5.5, via Theo (2026-09-25); adopted 2026-09-28.

- When a step doesn't need my input, keep going. Put status notes in the same message as your next action, never as a message on their own.
- Stop and ask only when you cannot continue without me, or before anything destructive: deleting data, force pushing, or changing anything outside the repo you were given.
- This narrows conversational check-ins, not the hard gates. The Constitution, the 3x scope-budget stop in `rules/agent-protocol.mdc`, and the no-push / no-PR lines in delegation briefs still apply unchanged.
- "Want me to continue?", a summary that names the next step without taking it, or a list of choices that don't block the work are not stops. Take the step.
- If something does not work as expected, say so and ask rather than guessing a workaround. Asking is cheaper than an hour down the wrong path.
- End every long run with four headings, in this order: **Blocked on you**, **Dangling**, **Changed**, **Found** (omit **Dangling** only when it would be empty) — then one last line: `Safe to exit: yes`, or `Safe to exit: no — <what is still running, roughly how long>` (ruled 2026-10-19 after nine "can I exit?" questions in two days).
- **Be very concise.** Facts only, no essay. Short bullets under each heading, one line each. No preamble, no restating the task, no narrating how you got there.
- **Found is not a parking lot (ruled 2026-10-13).** A real defect you discover mid-task, pre-existing or not, gets FIXED before the run ends: in the same PR when it is small and in the same area, otherwise in its own PR, opened and shepherded in the same run. Never write "pre-existing, not filed" or "judged, not changed" against a real bug. Found carries only observations that are not defects (test data left behind, behaviours judged correct, things not evaluated) and real defects that are genuinely too large to fix now, each of which then goes under **Dangling**, not under Found. If I had skipped the Found section, two inventory bugs would have been lost; that is the failure this rule exists to prevent.
- **Dangling is the only place a defect you did not fix may live (ruled 2026-10-31).** A 10-31 sweep found 44 closing notes since the 10-13 ruling and 26 PR bodies that parked real defects anyway ("untouched; a separate slice", "not changed; say the word", "## Not in this PR", "## Found, not changed", "## Question for Paul"); four sessions in one night each re-noted the same unfixed button. Paul: *"I don't check the notes every time … important issues might be raised in these comments and they're just going to be forgotten, and then we're just going to sit in the code until someone finds the bug."* And: *"If there's a dangling issue that honestly can't be dealt with at the time, then it needs to be raised in a separate section. It needs to be brought to my attention. It needs to be made sure that I can't miss it. I do usually read the 'blocked on me' section so have a section similar to that."* So: one line per defect under **Dangling**, directly after **Blocked on you**: what is broken, why it is not fixed in this run, and `Pick: <own PR | drop | needs your ruling>`; a `needs your ruling` pick ALSO puts the question itself, with options and a pick, under **Blocked on you** (Paul, 2026-10-31, after seven "Pick: your ruling" lines: *"what's blocked on me, give me the questions and options"*). A PR body, a spec, a run record, memory, a wiki page or a Found bullet is never the place; a PR body carries design decisions and what the slice IS, never a defect left behind or a question he has to find. Hooks: `found-is-not-a-parking-lot.js` (Stop) refuses a Found bullet or loose prose that defers, and a Dangling line with no pick; `block-parking-lot-prose.js` refuses deferral headings and vocabulary in PR text.

## Model Routing & Effort

The rubric — which model sits in which seat, effort settings, per-model quirks, and the routing rules — lives in `~/.claude/rubric/ROUTING.md`. **A session that is about to write code, delegate, choose a model, or judge a cheaper model's output reads it first.** An implementer or sub-agent that has been handed a task does not need it: the model was chosen when the task was handed over. Four lines bind everyone regardless: **the main session runs on Fable — orchestration and thinking are never done on Opus, Sonnet or Haiku; those are only ever delegates** (ruled 2026-10-17 after a day's audit, four PRs and three merges ran on Opus 5.5 because the launcher chose the model; **Opus 5.5 is the fallback for the main session only while the TeamClaude pool reports Fable at 100% on every account** (ruled 2026-10-21 D2 A: on 10-20 Paul switched to Opus himself when Fable ran out); the enforcing hook (`require-session-model.js`) was REMOVED on Paul's instruction 2026-11-01 — the rule is a routing preference, not a gate; nothing blocks a session on another model. Work done on Opus that way gets its assumptions re-checked on Fable afterwards — *"we did all this work on a dumber model, now we need to go back and recheck every assumption"* (10-21: the re-check found a wrong diagnosis sent to a merchant and three defects in merged PRs); **the re-check is a listed follow-up, never a merge gate** — an Opus session merges a green, walked PR under standing authority and lists every merge for the Fable re-check (ruled 2026-10-28 D2 A, after three Opus sessions on 10-27 read this line three ways and one parked three clean PRs for a day)); a Fable session edits code itself only for a change under ~20 lines in one file or when the edit is the taste call — everything larger is specced and delegated to Codex (Astra) or an Opus 5.5 Agent (ruled 2026-10-12); Claude effort stays on `high` (never `max`); and Codex is always pinned explicitly (`-m gpt-6-astra`, effort passed on the command line).

## Memory Budget (24 GB machine — added 2026-08-06 after three OOM wedges)

- **Test runs are the #1 memory killer.** Jest/vitest default to ~11 parallel node workers here; several sub-agents each launching a suite wedges the Mac. ALWAYS pass an explicit worker cap (`--maxWorkers=2`); run ONE suite at a time — never in parallel sub-agents or across worktrees. Enforced by PreToolUse hook `~/.claude/hooks/cap-test-workers.js` (blocks uncapped runs, and runs every capped suite under the machine-wide lock `~/.claude/scripts/with-test-lock.py` so a second suite WAITS — ruled 2026-10-30 D3 A; nothing to remember) and `VITEST_MAX_THREADS/FORKS=2` + `NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=2048` in settings env. For a genuinely huge build, override per-command: `NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=6144 <cmd>`.
- **Fan-out is split by what the helper does** (ruled 2026-08-31, replacing a flat cap of 3). Anything that builds or runs a suite is **strictly one at a time**, no exceptions. Read-only analysis and research agents get a much higher ceiling — judgement, not a number. An agent that looks read-only but shells out to a build counts as a build.
- **Docker VM is hard-capped at 3 GB** (hypervisor-enforced via `MemoryMiB` in Docker settings; AutoStart is off). Paul quits Docker Desktop when he's done with it; **a session must never quit the shared daemon** — it cannot see other sessions' containers, and doing so has corrupted the container store and cost an afternoon.
- **Watchdog backstop:** LaunchAgent `com.kilbot.memory-watchdog` (30 s interval) kills agent/test processes at memory-pressure warning/critical, before the machine wedges. Log: `~/.claude/logs/memory-watchdog.log`; status: `bash ~/.claude/scripts/memory-watchdog.sh --check`.

## Constants, Not Env Vars (all projects — added 2026-08-20)

AI models have a love affair with inventing environment variables. Stop. Nobody human reads or
sets them; the agent is the one who reads and edits the code, and infra auto-deploys on code
changes, so "tunable without a deploy" is a benefit nobody collects. Every invented env var adds
a parse/validate/fallback block, a compose/CI entry, and keep-in-step comments — layers guarding
a value that will only ever be changed by editing code anyway.

- **If it's a constant, write a named constant in code** with a comment saying why the value.
- Env vars are for exactly three things: **secrets/credentials**, **values that genuinely differ
  between environments running the same artifact** (paths, ports, URLs), and **values an outside
  system injects** (platform contracts).
- "Someone might tune it someday" is not a justification — when someday comes, edit the constant.
- When reviewing AI-written diffs (own or delegated), strike new env vars that don't meet this
  bar. This is a known failure mode of every model in the stack.
